stage: 4
finding: The gate assumes origin/main is the current landing base but neither establishes nor diagnoses that premise. In an isolated Git fixture running the unchanged script, after another seat's changelog-only commit is inherited while origin/main still points to its predecessor, a seat with no changelog changes of its own is refused (exit 1). Advancing origin/main to that inherited commit makes the identical tree pass. Conversely, with origin/main stale before an inherited 0.10.0 release bump, appending a forbidden per-change changelog entry passes (exit 0): the inherited bump supplies permission for a different change. The refusal tells the innocent seat to change package.version, not refresh/check its comparison base. Thus stale tracking refs can both block unrelated lands and fail to hold the same-change rule; the current pure-function tests do not cover the Git/base premise. Independently: clean gate exit 0, scratch changelog exit 1 restored; all four tests pass and each named claim's targeted mutation fails its own test. Removing semver.valid survives all four tests. Backward stable versions also pass, although stage 4 does not explicitly require monotonicity. Deletion/rename without bump refuse; 0.10.0 release passes; invalid version refuses. No full pre-land run; working tree restored.
